# CI/CD & Ops-Tooling Container Standards

This stack's "runtime container" is a CI/CD pipeline plus an optional Dockerized automation image — not an application server. This rule covers both.

## Part A: CI/CD Pipeline Pattern for Terraform

### Stage Order
```
fmt-check -> validate -> tflint -> checkov -> plan (PR comment) -> manual-approval gate -> apply (protected branch only) -> post-apply smoke check
```

Each stage gates the next. `apply` never runs on a PR — only on `main` (or a protected branch) after the plan has been reviewed and approved.

### Authentication: OIDC, Never Long-Lived Keys
CI authenticates to AWS via GitHub's OIDC provider assuming an IAM role — no `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`/`AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` stored as repo secrets:

```hcl
# modules/cicd/main.tf — one-time bootstrap of the OIDC trust
resource "aws_iam_openid_connect_provider" "github" {
  url             = "https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com"
  client_id_list  = ["sts.amazonaws.com"]
  thumbprint_list = ["6938fd4d98bab03faadb97b34396831e3780aea1"]
}

data "aws_iam_policy_document" "github_trust" {
  statement {
    actions = ["sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity"]
    principals {
      type        = "Federated"
      identifiers = [aws_iam_openid_connect_provider.github.arn]
    }
    condition {
      test     = "StringEquals"
      variable = "token.actions.githubusercontent.com:aud"
      values   = ["sts.amazonaws.com"]
    }
    condition {
      test     = "StringLike"
      variable = "token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub"
      values   = ["repo:acme/infra:ref:refs/heads/main"]
    }
  }
}

resource "aws_iam_role" "github_actions" {
  name               = "${local.name_prefix}-github-actions"
  assume_role_policy = data.aws_iam_policy_document.github_trust.json
}
```

### GitHub Actions Workflow Skeleton
```yaml
# .github/workflows/terraform.yml
name: Terraform

on:
  pull_request:
    paths: ["**.tf", "**.tfvars"]
  push:
    branches: [main]
    paths: ["**.tf", "**.tfvars"]

permissions:
  id-token: write   # required for OIDC
  contents: read
  pull-requests: write  # required to comment plan output on PRs

env:
  TF_VERSION: "1.9.8"
  AWS_REGION: "us-east-1"

jobs:
  validate:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: hashicorp/setup-terraform@v3
        with:
          terraform_version: ${{ env.TF_VERSION }}
      - run: terraform fmt -check -recursive
      - run: terraform init -backend=false
      - run: terraform validate
      - uses: terraform-linters/setup-tflint@v4
      - run: tflint --init && tflint

  checkov:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    needs: validate
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: bridgecrewio/checkov-action@master
        with:
          directory: .
          quiet: true

  plan:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    needs: checkov
    if: github.event_name == 'pull_request'
    permissions:
      id-token: write
      contents: read
      pull-requests: write
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v4
        with:
          role-to-assume: arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/acme-github-actions
          aws-region: ${{ env.AWS_REGION }}
      - uses: hashicorp/setup-terraform@v3
        with:
          terraform_version: ${{ env.TF_VERSION }}
      - run: terraform init
      - id: plan
        run: terraform plan -no-color -out=tfplan | tee plan_output.txt
      - uses: actions/github-script@v7
        with:
          script: |
            const fs = require('fs');
            const plan = fs.readFileSync('plan_output.txt', 'utf8').slice(0, 60000);
            github.rest.issues.createComment({
              issue_number: context.issue.number,
              owner: context.repo.owner,
              repo: context.repo.repo,
              body: `### Terraform Plan\n\`\`\`\n${plan}\n\`\`\``
            });

  apply:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    needs: checkov
    if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main' && github.event_name == 'push'
    environment: production   # requires manual approval via GitHub Environments
    permissions:
      id-token: write
      contents: read
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v4
        with:
          role-to-assume: arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/acme-github-actions
          aws-region: ${{ env.AWS_REGION }}
      - uses: hashicorp/setup-terraform@v3
        with:
          terraform_version: ${{ env.TF_VERSION }}
      - run: terraform init
      - run: terraform plan -out=tfplan
      - run: terraform apply -auto-approve tfplan
      - name: Post-apply smoke check
        run: |
          aws ecs describe-services --cluster acme-prod-cluster --services acme-prod-web \
            --query 'services[0].{running:runningCount,desired:desiredCount}'
```

The `environment: production` block ties into GitHub's **Environments** feature to require manual approval before `apply` runs — this is the "manual-approval gate" stage. See `templates/ci-pipeline-template.yml` for the copy-paste-ready version wired to `examples/aws-ecs-webapp`.

### GitLab CI Divergence (Notes Only)
GitLab CI achieves the same shape with `rules:` instead of `on:`, `environment:` + a manual job for the approval gate, and either OIDC (`id_tokens:`) or a CI/CD variable-scoped role. The stage order is identical: `validate -> tflint -> checkov -> plan -> apply (manual, protected branch)`.

## Part B: Dockerizing the Ops-Tooling CLI

Terraform itself is **not** typically containerized for local dev — engineers run it directly via `tfenv`. It CAN be pinned via a `Dockerfile.terraform` for reproducible CI runners if the course wants that pattern (see below). What IS commonly containerized is the **Python/boto3 automation CLI** (`scripts/deploy.py`, `scripts/verify_deployment.py`) when it needs to run outside a developer's machine — e.g. as an ECS scheduled task or a CI job image.

### Multi-Stage Dockerfile for the Ops CLI

```dockerfile
# Build stage
FROM python:3.11-slim AS builder
WORKDIR /app
COPY scripts/requirements.txt .
RUN pip install --user --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt

# Runtime stage
FROM python:3.11-slim
WORKDIR /app

# Create non-root user
RUN useradd --create-home --shell /bin/bash ops
USER ops

COPY --from=builder /root/.local /home/ops/.local
COPY --chown=ops:ops scripts/ .

ENV PATH=/home/ops/.local/bin:$PATH
ENV PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1
ENV PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1

ENTRYPOINT ["python", "deploy.py"]
```

Key differences from an application-server Dockerfile: no `EXPOSE`, no `HEALTHCHECK` (this is a CLI, not a long-running service), and the `ENTRYPOINT` is the Click command rather than a WSGI/ASGI server.

### Optional: Pinned Terraform Runner Image for CI Reproducibility

```dockerfile
# Dockerfile.terraform — only if the course wants pinned, containerized CI runners
FROM hashicorp/terraform:1.9.8

RUN apk add --no-cache python3 py3-pip aws-cli

WORKDIR /workspace
ENTRYPOINT ["terraform"]
```

Explicitly note to students: this is an optional reproducibility pattern for CI runners, not how Terraform is normally invoked in local development.

### `.dockerignore` for the Ops CLI Image
```
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
tests/
.terraform/
*.tfstate*
terraform_plans/
```
